maak (0.19.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The task description is data, and every consumer reads the same one.
    `maak --list --json` prints it as one versioned JSON object: each task's
    docstring, the line its definition starts on, what it needs first, an
    example invocation, and each argument with its kind, default and prose.
  * `task-metadata!` declares what a task is beyond its docstring, in exactly
    three fields: `#:depends`, `#:hidden?` and `#:example`.  A dependency orders
    a run, sequential or -j, and nothing else; nothing is remembered between
    runs.
  * Argument prose comes from an `Args:` section in the docstring, which is the
    convention the library's own docstrings already kept.
  * contrib/bin/maak-tasks is a front end for maak's own reader rather than a
    second parser of the same file, so the helper and `maak --list --json`
    agree byte for byte on a file whose tasks are written out.
  * A parameter is no longer a task: `maak-proc?` no longer counts a value that
    happens to be callable.

 -- Josep Bigorra <jjbigorra@gmail.com>  Sat, 14 Nov 2026 22:00:00 +0100

maak (0.18.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The README has an Editor support section, with maak.el named as the
    canonical front end, and its list of projects that run maak moved to
    docs/who-uses-maak.org.  docs/editors.org leads with Emacs and lists
    maak.el beside the seven integrations that ship in contrib/.

 -- Josep Bigorra <jjbigorra@gmail.com>  Tue, 10 Nov 2026 19:40:00 +0100

maak (0.18.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The generated Zed task file is kept in step with the Maak file:
    contrib/zed/maak-zed sync writes it after an edit and watch writes it
    whenever the file changes.  The generated file carries the watch as a task
    of its own.

 -- Josep Bigorra <jjbigorra@gmail.com>  Tue, 10 Nov 2026 19:05:00 +0100

maak (0.17.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Editor integrations under contrib/, for Neovim, Vim, VS Code, Helix,
    IntelliJ, Zed and Sublime Text, all reading the task list from one Guile
    helper.  Every one of them can dry-run a task, and Neovim, Vim and VS Code
    repeat the last run.

 -- Josep Bigorra <jjbigorra@gmail.com>  Tue, 10 Nov 2026 18:30:00 +0100

